COMMIT eb15a31eee9c971e8ed8f57c536e4372db53c6ea
fix(core): a failing park no longer replaces the failure it was recovering from

Parking.wrap recovers a step failure by parking it in the DLQ. Two catches were too narrow, so a
throwable out of the PARK ITSELF - an Error, or a checked one thrown sneakily by an interrupted
await - escaped and REPLACED the step's own failure, with nothing recorded anywhere. That is
FIX-140's shape one layer out: the recovery path destroying what it was recovering from. Both
catches take Throwable now, the park call sits inside the try that recovers from it, and the park's
own failure is suppressed on the step's failure, which still leaves.

Parking.java:49 is deliberately untouched. D-111 settles it: it catches Exception only and must keep
doing so, because parking an Error would make it transient, which D-098 refused.

Two changes beyond the two catch sites, said here so neither reads as a silent extra. A guard
against self-suppression, because addSuppressed(self) throws IllegalArgumentException and would
itself replace the failure - FIX-140's shape a third time, in the fix for it. And the interrupt flag
is restored before the failure is rethrown. Both are pinned; the flag by cases that already existed,
though not by the one I first claimed - my grep found three cases asserting the flag and one of them
drives the outer catch, which never reaches the new code. The reviewer traced which catch each case
enters and found it pinned by the other two.

Review r1 failed on my own blocker: the moved park call was unpinned, and the comment above it
claimed two cases pinned it that cannot. With persist catching Throwable, a throwable out of it
becomes a failed future the await rethrows from either position, so the position was untested. What
separates the positions is that Parked.of reads failure.toString() (Parked.java:49) before any
future exists, so the case that pins it is a step failure whose toString() throws. Writing that case
hit a trap worth recording: opentest4j guards its eager toString with catch (Exception), so an Error
out of a value's toString() escapes the assertion formatter and the run reports Errors: 1 instead of
Failures: 1, even with a literal message. Assertion order is the fix. The comment now separates the
width of the catch from the position of the call, with its own witness for each.

Checks: all ten mutants killed (agent/work/FIX-142-mutants.log), each with its diff logged before the
run and cmp clean after a cp restore. B killed three cases where two were predicted, the extra being
the new case, and the verifier reported the mismatch rather than trimming it to the prediction. I
killed only the sneaky-checked twin, which is the third task running to show that a
RuntimeException-or-Error multicatch would not do here. VERIFIED_PASS integrated on 94b6d02:
ParkingTest 22, ProducersCloseTest 19, whole reactor clean verify -Pit exit 0 BUILD SUCCESS across
all 17 modules, flottila-core 563, coverage_check exit 0 with no decrease. The known FIX-144 flake
did not occur in this run.

Also in this commit: FIX-144's row corrected twice and its measurement recorded - the flake is real
and load-dependent, idle 0/20 against loaded 1/20, and the elapsed time I first reasoned from carries
no information, since passing and failing runs both take about 10.15 s. FIX-148 opened for what that
measurement exposed and then corrected from the Surefire XML with no new build: the self-test class
costs 39.6 s per run in expired two-second patiences, while a healthy run of the whole contract takes
0.025 s. FIX-143's count settled at ten, with Parking.java:49 struck because D-111 had already
settled it - a mistake the ledger had recorded once and I made again. FIX-146 and FIX-147 opened.

